course: dictionary
lesson: 1833
title: Coax: gentle persuasion
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: Coax is a verb. To coax someone is to persuade them gently and patiently, not by forcing them. Think of coaxing a shy cat out from under a bed: you do not drag it out, you wait and call to it softly.
explain: The word shows kind persuasion. «An enormous puppy reaches out a paw to her; Alice speaks to it "in a coaxing tone", gently and sweetly.» Gentle and sweet are the clues, so coaxing is soft and patient, not loud or rough.
explain: Coax can also mean to get something working by careful, gentle effort, like «coax the fire into life». Ask yourself: is someone or something being encouraged gently and patiently? If yes, coax probably fits.
fact: Coax means to persuade gently and patiently.
fact: You can coax a person, such as «coaxing a younger child to eat vegetables».
fact: Coax can mean to get something to work by careful, gentle effort, such as «coax the fire into life».
fact: Useful phrases: «coax out», «coax into», «coax a smile».
fact: Coax is also a short form of «coaxial cable», a TV or aerial cable. This is a technical use, so it is a different kind of word.
exercise: In «He tries coaxing it, slapping it, punching it», which words show force, the opposite of coaxing? | Laughing and running | Coaxing only | *Slapping and punching | Sleeping and dreaming
exercise: «Alice speaks to it in a coaxing tone.» What does a coaxing tone sound like? | Fast and cheerful | Loud and angry | Bored and tired | *Gentle and sweet
exercise: Dad wants to coax his little sister to eat her peas. What is he trying to do? | Throw the peas away | Hide the peas | *Gently persuade her | Force her to eat them
test: «The mother has to coax them in» means the mother gets her nervous otter cubs into the water. What does coax mean here? | *Gently persuade them to go in | Splash them with water | Force them into the water roughly | Leave them on the bank
test: What does «coax a smile» mean? | Make someone laugh loudly | Frown at someone | Smile very widely | *Gently get someone to smile
exam: Which sentence uses coax correctly? | The wind coaxed the door shut with a loud bang. | *Ben coaxed his nervous puppy out from under the table with a biscuit. | Mum coaxed a loud argument in the kitchen. | Ben shouted coax at the puppy until it ran away.
